I was under the impression that the CV is defined as the ratio of the standard deviation (or standard error) to the mean (sigma/mu) ?

Therefore the CV should be undefined if the mean is equal to zero?

Chris

From: distance...@googlegroups.com [mailto:distance...@googlegroups.com] On Behalf Of Eric Rexstad
Sent: November 16, 2016 11:16 AM
To: Dave Fifield; distance-sampling
Subject: Re: [distance-sampling] Interpretation of zero variance from dsm.var.gam()

Dave

Short answer:  CV is defined as point estimate (in this case estimated density) divided by SE of that estimate.

If density estimate is zero, then CV will be zero.  If SE is zero, CV is undefined.

Seems your white areas coincide with no detections, implying density is zero; eh?

